tip: | Look at the tree. | A trunk, a crown of ovals, and a shadow on the ground. It stands on a tile: the base of the trunk is on the center of a cell.
tip: 1 | Big shapes first. | The whole silhouette in one color. A tree reads from its outline: wide crown, narrow trunk, a bit of flare at the ground.
tip: 3 | Each oval has its own light. | Top left of every cluster is light, and the bottom right falls into the next one. The lowest row is the darkest, as the crown shades itself.
tip: 4 | Bark comes last. | The trunk is three vertical bands, wider at the base. A few dark marks are enough.
tip: | The shadow is its own layer. | It falls away from the light and stays at 35 percent, so it can change without touching the tree. It came from Cast shadow.
